\subsection{DP in Machine Learning}

Beyond SGD and deep learning, DP has been applied across many machine learning tasks:
- \textbf{Private Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM):} The general problem of minimizing $\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i} L(w; z_i)$ (empirical risk) under DP was studied by Chaudhuri et al. \cite{Chaudhuri2011}, Bassily et al. \cite{Bassily2014}, and many others. Techniques include:
  - \textit{Output perturbation:} Compute the optimal model on the whole data (e.g. by gradient descent until convergence) and then add noise to the model parameters before release. This can work if the model is stable and strongly convex so that one data point doesn’t shift the optimum too much.
  - \textit{Objective perturbation:} Add a random linear term to the objective function $L(w;D) + \langle b, w \rangle$ with $b$ drawn from some distribution. Solve this modified objective to get $w^*$. This yields a different solution but one that is close to the original optimum while achieving DP. This method was introduced by \cite{Chaudhuri2011}.
  - \textit{Gradient perturbation (DP-SGD):} Which we already discussed.
  - These methods have theoretical guarantees on the excess risk (difference between private model's loss and true minimal loss). For convex losses, roughly we see an $O(1/n)$ or $O(1/\sqrt{n})$ degradation depending on $\epsilon$ scaling. For example, Bassily et al. \cite{Bassily2014} showed optimal algorithms where the error due to privacy is $O\Big(\frac{\text{(dimension)} \cdot \ln(1/\delta)}{n \epsilon}\Big)$ for convex ERM.
- \textbf{Transfer learning and knowledge transfer:} PATE (Private Aggregation of Teacher Ensembles) by Papernot et al. is a technique where multiple teacher models are trained on disjoint subsets of sensitive data (non-DP), and then their predictions are aggregated with noise to label a student model's training set. This aggregation is differentially private. The student model learns from these noisy aggregated labels and can be released. PATE is not exactly an SGD method, but a form of DP model distillation which has been used for tasks like private image classification.
- \textbf{Clustering:} Algorithms for k-means or k-median clustering under DP exist. Typically, one uses exponential mechanism to pick cluster centers that approximately minimize the clustering cost.
- \textbf{Feature selection and dimension reduction:} DP versions of PCA (principal component analysis) or selecting top features have been developed. For PCA, one can either add noise to the covariance matrix and then compute eigenvectors, or use iterative algorithms with noise. The challenge is to preserve the structure while adding noise.

In summary, most standard ML tasks (regression, classification, clustering, etc.) have been studied under the DP lens. The general pattern is: ensure any single data point has limited influence (by regularization, clipping, etc.) and add noise to mask that influence. The result is often a trade-off: the model might need more data to reach the same accuracy as a non-private model due to the noise.

